1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:07,000
Most Shopify stores pay every month for apps nobody uses anymore.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:13,400
Let's walk through how that happens, and how to find them.

3
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:22,000
This is what your customers see: products, a cart, and a few helpful widgets.

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Each of those widgets comes from an app someone installed.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:39,400
But the storefront only tells part of the story. The rest lives in your admin.

6
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:49,000
Now look behind the counter. This is the app list in an example store's admin.

7
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Fourteen apps, and every one of them bills every month.

8
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:09,400
The question nobody asks: which of these are still doing anything?

9
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:16,500
Nobody installed these by mistake.

10
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:23,000
The founder added the basics at launch.

11
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:29,000
The marketing manager added a timer, a popup and a loyalty trial for Black Friday.

12
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:34,500
An agency added a page builder during the redesign. A freelancer added heatmaps to fix tracking.

13
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:45,400
Then each project ended. The apps stayed. So did the monthly bills.

14
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:52,000
Sort them by what they actually do today.

15
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:58,000
Six still earn their keep.

16
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Eight are idle. A timer for a sale that ended. A popup nobody sees. Heatmaps nobody opens. A loyalty program that never launched.

17
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:19,400
Idle apps don't stop billing. That's bloat, and it adds up every single month.

18
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:24,000
That bloat comes in three kinds.

19
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:31,000
Idle: installed and billing, but not doing anything anymore.

20
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:39,000
Leftover: switched off, but its code can still load on your pages and slow them down.

21
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:47,400
Doubled: two tools doing one job, like two reviews apps.

22
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:56,000
GitHub Scout starts with a free check. Paste your store address.

23
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:05,000
It looks at your store the way a shopper's browser does, and lists the apps it can see loading.

24
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:11,000
It flags tools doing the same job, and shows what's still loading, like a timer from a sale that ended.

25
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:17,400
It can't see apps that only run inside your admin. We say so up front.

26
00:03:18,500 --> 00:03:26,000
The reviewed audit adds the part only you can see: your app list from Shopify admin.

27
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:33,000
A person lines the two lists up.

28
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:39,000
Billing, but not showing up anywhere? Ask what it's for.

29
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:43,400
Loading on your pages, but gone from your list? That's leftover code.

30
00:03:44,500 --> 00:03:53,000
You get one page back: what to cut, what to keep, what to swap, and what to test.

31
00:03:53,000 --> 00:04:01,400
Read by a person. No app to install. And we don't sell any of the apps we recommend.

32
00:04:02,500 --> 00:04:05,000
Then cut safely.

33
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:14,000
Export what you need. Switch it off for one billing cycle.

34
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:25,400
Check your theme for code it left behind. Then cancel.

35
00:04:26,500 --> 00:04:33,000
Find the bloat. Keep what works.

36
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:41,400
Run the free check on your store. The link is right below this video.
